Robot <-> PC protocol
================================================================

Transport: TCP. The PC is the SERVER (port 3000), the ESP32 is the CLIENT
The robot reconnects by itself when the connection drops.


ROBOT -> PC
----------------------------
Every loop (~50 ms) the robot writes, without extra framing:

  0. RESPONSE packets (0x52), then a STATE packet (0x53) - only when there is something to send
  1. raw LD06 lidar UART bytes  - whatever the UART had buffered; frames CAN be
                                  split between loops
  2. VELOCITY packet (0x56, 13 B)
  3. ANGLE packet    (0x41, 13 B)   - always right after VELOCITY

The PC must scan the stream byte by byte and validate each candidate.
VELOCITY is only accepted together with the ANGLE packet right after it (the PC reads the pair).
0x52 / 0x53 are only accepted at a loop start: right after ANGLE, after another
RESPONSE/STATE or as the first bytes of a connection (lidar bytes can contain the same values).

LIDAR frame (LD06), 47 B:
  0x54                header
  0x2C                VerLen (12 points)
  uint16 speed        deg/s
  uint16 startAngle   0.01 deg
  12 x { uint16 distance [mm], uint8 confidence }
  uint16 endAngle     0.01 deg
  uint16 timestamp    ms, wraps at 30000
  uint8  crc8         CRC8 (poly 0x4D) over the previous 46 bytes

VELOCITY packet, 13 B - Kalman estimate from robot/main/Kalman/MotionEstimator (50 Hz):
  0x56
  float  velocity               m/s along the direction of travel, starts from 0 each move
  uint64 time                   us since the robot booted, time of the estimate

ANGLE packet, 13 B:
  0x41
  float  heading                rad since boot, CLOCKWISE positive, kept across moves (not wrapped)
  uint64 time                   us since the robot booted (read separately from VELOCITY's,
                                can be one estimation step newer)

RESPONSE packet, 13 B - answer to one Request, when it is finished or rejected:
  0x52
  uint32 id                     id of the Request
  uint32 status                 0 = succeeded, 1 = failed (rejected: queue full / FAILURE state /
                                unknown type / powerMode below 100)
  uint32 time                   ms the robot executed it

STATE packet, 5 B - on every change and every 10 s:
  0x53
  uint32 state                  0 Idle, 1 Calibrating, 2 Ready, 3 Processing, 4 Failure
  The RESPONSE of a finished request is always sent before the following state change.


PC -> ROBOT
-----------------------------
REQUEST packet, 18 B (packed):
  0x51                  header; the robot skips bytes until it sees it
  uint32 type           0 = move, 1 = rotate
  float  distanceInM    used by move   (negative = backwards)
  float  rotationAngle  used by rotate (degrees)
  uint32 id             returned in the RESPONSE; the PC uses 1, 2, 3, ... (never 0)
  uint8  powerMode      PWM duty x/255: 100 ultraSlow, 150 slow, 180 normal, 200 mediumFast,
                        220 fast, 255 overdrive. Below 100 the robot answers failed.

The robot queues up to 8 requests and executes them one at a time, sensor data keeps flowing.
The PC (RobotLink) sends the next request only after the RESPONSE of the previous one.
